## The Numbers Don't Lie

As we inch closer to the halfway mark of the year, the food industry has already racked up an unsettling 419 recalls. To put that in perspective, that's over half of the total 777 historical recalls tracked. Class I recalls, the most severe type, account for nearly a quarter of this year's numbers at 103 cases so far. These figures are clear indicators that there's still work to be done to ensure food safety.

## Repeat Offenders

Brands like Moonlight Packing Corporation, Albertsons Companies LLC, and Diva Fam Inc are among the most frequent offenders. It begs the question: why are these companies consistently failing to meet safety standards? For instance, Moonlight Packing Corporation and Albertsons scored a dismal 96 and 94 respectively, both earning an unflattering 'F' grade on their safety report cards. Such repeated lapses are a sour note in the melody of consumer trust.

## The Latest Blunders

Let's take a look at the latest recalls. On May 13, 2026, Silver Moon LP dba Loard's Ice Cream issued multiple recalls due to undeclared allergens in their popular ice cream flavors. Meanwhile, California Dairies Inc. is facing a Class I recall for potential Salmonella contamination in their milk powders. Yet another Class I recall was announced by Raw Farm LLC due to an E. coli outbreak linked to their raw cheddar cheese. The recurrence of such issues highlights systemic failures in quality control.

## Culprit Categories

Among the 419 recalls this year, allergens lead with 240 cases. Salmonella follows with 112, and Listeria is not far behind at 114. Perhaps most concerning is the 85 cases of foreign objects found in food--proof that even basic manufacturing practices are often neglected.

## What Can Be Done?

While consumers can stay vigilant by checking for recall notices, it's clear that systemic changes are needed. Stricter regulations, increased penalties for repeat offenders, and a cultural shift towards prioritizing safety over profits can help turn this trend around. Until then, consider this a reminder to always be cautious about what you put on your plate.
